  "worked_solution": "We model the mass of the sample using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nR(t)={R}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${R}_{0}$ is the initial mass.\n\nWe're told that the initial mass of the sample is $80$ milligrams, so we have ${R}_{0}=80.$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the sample $R$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nR(t)=80\\,{e}^{-0.05t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the mass of the sample when $t=30,$ we substitute $t=30$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(30) & =80\\,{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 30} \\\\  & =80\\,{e}^{-1.5} \\\\  & =17.850\u2026\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the mass of the sample after $30$ days is $18$ milligrams.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
